Carried patch, Windows only: LIBXSMM's transposed-A GEMM under the Win64 ABI.

For a GEMM with TRANS_A (F32 or F64), the x86 generator copies A, transposed,
into a stack buffer and repoints the A register at it. That happens between
a push and a pop of the registers named by gp_save_bitmask. On Linux the A
register is RDI, which the mask leaves out, so the new pointer survives. On
Windows the A register is RCX, the Win64 argument register, which the mask
saves, so the pop puts back the original A and the kernel multiplies the
untransposed matrix with the transposed leading dimension: wrong values, no
crash. libCEED's xsmm backends ask for TRANS_A in every untransposed
contraction with C == 1, such as interpolating a basis on one element. The
register being repointed is now left out of the mask. Still present on
LIBXSMM main.

--- a/src/generator_gemm_common.c
+++ b/src/generator_gemm_common.c
@@ -291,6 +291,9 @@ LIBXSMM_API_INTERN void libxsmm_generator_gemm_setup_A_vnni_or_trans_B_vnni_or_t
   unsigned int l_trans_ldi = i_xgemm_desc_orig->lda;
   unsigned int l_trans_ldo = i_xgemm_desc_orig->m;
 
+  /* l_reg_a is repointed at the transposed copy on the stack below, so it must not be
+   * restored. On Windows it is RCX, which the mask saves; on Linux it is RDI, which it does not. */
+  gp_save_bitmask = (unsigned short)(gp_save_bitmask & ~(1u << l_reg_a));
   libxsmm_generator_x86_save_gpr_regs( io_generated_code, gp_save_bitmask);
 
   if ( (i_micro_kernel_config->atvnni_gemm_stack_alloc_tensors !=0) || (i_micro_kernel_config->atvnni_btrans_gemm_stack_alloc_tensors !=0) ) {
